<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- Telling-and-practice activity: derivatives of polynomials.
     Arc: observe in Desmos (Section 1), name the operator and the
     observed rules, then practice (Section 2), then use the formulas
     to find critical points by factoring (Section 3).

     Timing: aim for about 30 minutes of material. Section 1 is short
     on purpose (about 8 to 10 minutes with whole-class debrief).
     Sections 2 and 3 are mostly telling plus practice. If the period
     runs long, trim Section 2 tasks 3 and 4, then Section 3 task 5.

     Desmos: students use the plain calculator at desmos.com/calculator.
     Desmos computes f'(x) directly once f(x) is defined, so no saved
     graph or QR image is needed. A saved page with sliders could be
     added later following activities 05 and 06.

     Answer key
     Section 1: f'(x) = m for mx+b; x^2 gives 2x; x^3 gives 3x^2;
       x^4 gives 4x^3. Degree drops by one. Conjecture n x^(n-1).
       3x^2 gives 6x; x^2+5 gives 2x; x^3+x^2 gives 3x^2+2x.
     Section 2: 30x^4 - 12x^2; 4x^3 + 6x - 9; 2x^3 - 2x^2;
       (x+2)(x-3) = x^2 - x - 6 gives 2x - 1;
       f(x) = x^3 - 6x^2 + 5 has f'(x) = 3x^2 - 12x, f'(1) = -9,
       f'(4) = 0.
     Section 3 worked example: 3x^2 - 12x = 3x(x-4), x = 0, 4.
       Task 1: 3x^2 - 12 = 3(x-2)(x+2), x = -2, 2.
       Task 2: 6x^2 - 18x + 12 = 6(x-1)(x-2), x = 1, 2.
       Task 3: 4x^3 - 12x^2 = 4x^2(x-3), x = 0, 3.
       Task 4: 3x^2 - 6x - 3 = 3(x^2 - 2x - 1), x = 1 +/- sqrt(2).
       Task 5: 3x^2 + 3 = 3(x^2+1), no real roots, no critical points.
     Task 3 has a repeated factor x^2 and task 2 has two simple
     factors. These are deliberately set up for sign analysis of f'(x)
     in the next activity, so students should keep the factored forms. -->
<worksheet xml:id="activity-polynomial-derivative">
  <title>Derivatives of Polynomials and Critical Points</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Last time we defined the derivative <m>f'(x)</m> as the function whose output at each <m>x</m>-value is the slope of the tangent line to the graph of <m>f(x)</m>.
      We found it by drawing tangent lines and estimating slopes, which is slow and imprecise.
    </p>
    <p>
      Today we use technology to look for patterns in the derivatives of polynomials, and we state those patterns as rules that give exact formulas.
      Then we use the formulas to find the <m>x</m>-values where the graph of <m>f(x)</m> has a horizontal tangent line.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-polyderiv-observe" workspace="0in">
    <title>Section 1: Observing derivatives in Desmos</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Open the Desmos graphing calculator at
        <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator" visual="desmos.com/calculator"/>
        and follow these steps.
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li><p>On line 1, enter <c>f(x)=mx+b</c>. When Desmos offers to add sliders, choose <q>all</q>.</p></li>
        <li><p>On line 2, enter <c>f'(x)</c>. Desmos graphs the derivative of <m>f(x)</m> for you. (Type the apostrophe next to the Enter key.)</p></li>
        <li><p>Turn on both graphs and zoom out until you can see the overall shape of each.</p></li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        Keep the graph of <m>f(x)</m> and the graph of <m>f'(x)</m> in mind as two different functions.
        The first reports heights and the second reports slopes of the first.
      </p>
      <table xml:id="table-polyderiv-observations">
        <title>Derivatives observed in Desmos</title>
        <tabular halign="center">
          <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
            <cell><m>f(x)</m></cell>
            <cell><m>f'(x)</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><m>mx+b</m></cell>
            <cell/>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><m>x^2</m></cell>
            <cell/>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><m>x^3</m></cell>
            <cell/>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><m>x^4</m></cell>
            <cell/>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>
    </introduction>
    <task xml:id="task-polyderiv-linear" workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Move the sliders for <m>m</m> and <m>b</m> and watch the graph of <m>f'(x)</m>.
          What formula describes <m>f'(x)</m> for <m>f(x)=mx+b</m>?
          Record it in <xref ref="table-polyderiv-observations"/>, and explain why this makes sense for the graph of a line.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-polyderiv-powers" workspace="0.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Change line 1 to <c>f(x)=x^2</c>, then <c>f(x)=x^3</c>, then <c>f(x)=x^4</c>.
          For each one, use the graph of <m>f'(x)</m> to decide what its formula is, and record it in <xref ref="table-polyderiv-observations"/>.
          To check a guess, enter it on line 3 (for example <c>g(x)=2x</c>) and see whether its graph lands exactly on the graph of <m>f'(x)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-polyderiv-conjecture" workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <ol marker="a.">
          <li><p>Compare the degree of each <m>f(x)</m> in the table with the degree of its derivative <m>f'(x)</m>. What do you notice?</p></li>
          <li><p>Make a conjecture: if <m>f(x)=x^n</m>, then <m>f'(x)=\underline{\qquad\qquad}</m>. Predict <m>f'(x)</m> for <m>f(x)=x^5</m> and check your prediction in Desmos.</p></li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-polyderiv-combine" workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Your conjecture is only about <m>x^n</m> on its own.
          Predict, then check in Desmos, the derivative of each function below.
        </p>
        <ol marker="a.">
          <li><p><m>f(x)=3x^2</m></p></li>
          <li><p><m>f(x)=x^2+5</m></p></li>
          <li><p><m>f(x)=x^3+x^2</m></p></li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          In words, what happens to a coefficient, to an added constant, and to a sum of terms when we take the derivative?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-polyderiv-rules" workspace="0in">
    <title>Section 2: Derivative formulas</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Taking a derivative is an operation we perform on a formula, just as taking a limit is.
        We give it an operator symbol.
      </p>

      <assemblage>
        <title>The Differentiation Operator</title>
        <p>
          The notation <m>\dfrac{d}{dx}\left[ u \right]</m> means <q>the derivative of the formula <m>u</m> with respect to <m>x</m></q>.
          If <m>f(x)</m> is a function, then
          <md>
            <mrow>f'(x) = \frac{d}{dx}\left[ f(x) \right]</mrow>
          </md>.
          The operator acts on a formula, so we can use it without giving the formula a function name.
        </p>
      </assemblage>

      <p>
        What you saw in Desmos can be stated as rules.
        These are observed patterns for now.
        We will prove them once we have the definition of the derivative.
      </p>

      <assemblage>
        <title>The Power Rule</title>
        <p>
          For any positive integer <m>n</m>,
          <md>
            <mrow>\frac{d}{dx}\left[ x^n \right] = n x^{n-1}</mrow>
          </md>.
          In particular <m>\dfrac{d}{dx}\left[ x \right] = 1</m>.
          For a constant <m>k</m>, the graph of <m>y=k</m> is a horizontal line, so <m>\dfrac{d}{dx}\left[ k \right] = 0</m>.
        </p>
      </assemblage>

      <assemblage>
        <title>The Sum Rule and the Constant Multiple Rule</title>
        <p>
          Let <m>u</m> and <m>v</m> be formulas and let <m>k</m> be a constant.
          <md>
            <mrow>\frac{d}{dx}\left[ u + v \right] \amp = \frac{d}{dx}\left[ u \right] + \frac{d}{dx}\left[ v \right]</mrow>
            <mrow>\frac{d}{dx}\left[ k\,u \right] \amp = k\,\frac{d}{dx}\left[ u \right]</mrow>
          </md>.
          Subtraction is covered by these two rules, since <m>u - v = u + (-1)v</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          We often use both the sum and constant multiple rules in a single step.
          When combined, it is often called the <term>linearity</term> of the derivative.
          Suppose <m>c_1</m> and <m>c_2</m> are any constants and <m>u</m> and <m>v</m> represent formulas:
          <md>
            \frac{d}{dx}\left[ c_1 u + c_2 v \right] = c_1 \frac{d}{dx}[u] + c_2 \frac{d}{dx}[v]
          </md>
          
        </p>
        <p>
          
        </p>
      </assemblage>

      <p>
        Together, these let us differentiate any polynomial one term at a time.
        <md>
          <mrow>\frac{d}{dx}\left[ 5x^4 - 3x^2 + 7x - 2 \right] \amp = 5\,\frac{d}{dx}\left[ x^4 \right] - 3\,\frac{d}{dx}\left[ x^2 \right] + 7\,\frac{d}{dx}\left[ x \right] - \frac{d}{dx}\left[ 2 \right]</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp = 5(4x^3) - 3(2x) + 7(1) - 0</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp = 20x^3 - 6x + 7</mrow>
        </md>.
        With practice you will skip the middle line and write the answer directly.
        Notice that the degree of the polynomial dropped by one, as you observed.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task xml:id="task-polyderiv-practice-1" workspace="0.75in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>\displaystyle \frac{d}{dx}\left[ 6x^5 - 4x^3 \right]</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-polyderiv-practice-2" workspace="0.75in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>\displaystyle \frac{d}{dx}\left[ x^4 + 3x^2 - 9x + 8 \right]</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-polyderiv-practice-fractions" workspace="0.75in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>\displaystyle \frac{d}{dx}\left[ \tfrac{1}{2}x^4 - \tfrac{2}{3}x^3 + 5 \right]</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-polyderiv-practice-expand" workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>\displaystyle \frac{d}{dx}\left[ (x+2)(x-3) \right]</m>
        </p>
        <p>
          The rules above do not tell us how to differentiate a product of two formulas.
          What must you do to the formula first?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-polyderiv-practice-slopes" workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>f(x)=x^3-6x^2+5</m>.
        </p>
        <ol marker="a.">
          <li><p>Find <m>f'(x)</m>.</p></li>
          <li><p>Compute <m>f'(1)</m> and <m>f'(4)</m>. Write a formula for the tangent line of <m>f(x)</m> at each point.</p></li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-polyderiv-critical" workspace="0in">
    <title>Section 3: Critical points</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        In the last task you found that <m>f'(4)=0</m>, so the graph of <m>f(x)</m> has a horizontal tangent line at <m>x=4</m>.
        The <m>x</m>-values where the slope is zero, or where there is no slope exists, are the places where the behavior of a graph can change direction.
        We give them a name.
      </p>

      <assemblage>
        <title>Critical Points</title>
        <p>
          A <term>critical point</term> of <m>f(x)</m> is an <m>x</m>-value <m>x=a</m> in the domain of <m>f(x)</m> where either <m>f'(a)=0</m> or <m>f'(a)</m> does not exist.
        </p>
      </assemblage>

      <p>
        The graph with the corner from last class has a critical point at its corner, because the tangent line does not exist there.
        For a polynomial, however, <m>f'(x)</m> is again a polynomial, so it exists for every <m>x</m>.
        That means the critical points of a polynomial are exactly the roots of <m>f'(x)</m>.
      </p>

      <assemblage>
        <title>Finding Critical Points of a Polynomial</title>
        <p>
          <ol>
            <li><p>Compute <m>f'(x)</m> using the derivative rules.</p></li>
            <li><p>Factor <m>f'(x)</m> completely. Pull out the greatest common factor first, including any constant, and then factor what remains.</p></li>
            <li><p>A product is zero only when one of its factors is zero. Set each factor containing <m>x</m> equal to zero and solve.
              <ul>
                <li><p>A constant factor is never zero, so it adds no roots.</p></li>
                <li><p>If a quadratic factor does not factor, use the quadratic formula.</p></li>
              </ul></p></li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </assemblage>

      <p>
        For <m>f(x)=x^3-6x^2+5</m> we found <m>f'(x)=3x^2-12x</m>, and so
        <md>
          <mrow>f'(x) \amp = 3x^2-12x = 3x(x-4)</mrow>
          <mrow>3x(x-4) = 0 \amp \quad\Longrightarrow\quad x=0 \text{ or } x=4</mrow>
        </md>.
        The critical points of <m>f(x)</m> are <m>x=0</m> and <m>x=4</m>.
        Keep the factored form of <m>f'(x)</m>. Next we will use it to decide where <m>f'(x)</m> is positive and negative.
      </p>

      <p>
        For each function below, answer the following.
        <ol marker="a.">
          <li><p>Find <m>f'(x)</m>.</p></li>
          <li><p>Factor <m>f'(x)</m> completely.</p></li>
          <li><p>Solve <m>f'(x)=0</m> and list the critical points of <m>f(x)</m>.</p></li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task xml:id="task-polyderiv-critical-1" workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>f(x)=x^3-12x+4</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-polyderiv-critical-2" workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>f(x)=2x^3-9x^2+12x+1</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-polyderiv-critical-3" workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>f(x)=x^4-4x^3+7</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-polyderiv-critical-quadratic-formula" workspace="2in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>f(x)=x^3-3x^2-3x+4</m>
        </p>
        <p>
          After pulling out the common factor, the remaining quadratic does not factor with integers.
          Use the quadratic formula to find its roots.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-polyderiv-critical-none" workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>f(x)=x^3+3x</m>
        </p>
        <p>
          How many critical points does <m>f(x)</m> have?
          What does that tell you about the tangent lines to its graph?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-polyderiv-practice-web" workspace="0in">
    <title>Section 4: More Practice with Computer Feedback</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Practice computing derivatives of polynomials and other algebraic formulas with similar problems at the web page below.
      </p>
      <image source="images/qr-derivative-practice.png" width="20%" margins="0% 80%">
        <description>
          <p>QR code linking to the derivative practice web page.</p>
        </description>
      </image>
      <p>
        <url href="https://dbrianwalton.github.io/home/webapp/derivs_algebraic.html"
             visual="dbrianwalton.github.io/home/webapp/derivs_algebraic.html"/>
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task xml:id="task-polyderiv-practice-web-notes" workspace="2in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Use this space to record one problem from the practice page and your work on it.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>
</worksheet>
